  "notes": "Logo is the full-size 1200 by 1200 round black logo (EAC-New-Logo-2021-Round-Black-FINAL.png) shown in the site footer and on the about page; the page header and site icon use a cropped copy of the same logo (cropped-EAC-New-Logo-2021-Round-Black-FINAL.png). No videos: the site embeds no YouTube, Vimeo or video files and its social links are Facebook, Instagram, Flickr and TripAdvisor only, so videos is empty. Tour photos are also offered on the operator's Flickr, which was not opened. heroImage left out: the facts file already uses EAC-Hero-2023-Opt.jpg (a guest paddling a sea kayak), and kayak-aerial-2-scaled.jpg (Dampier Archipelago aerial, 2560 wide) is the alternative to compare. Gallery leads with the site's hero slider photos, then trip photos from the home page. Photo-to-trip matches follow the page each photo sits on, because the site reuses image descriptions across unrelated pages (for example the Modern-History-Heritage-Main file is described as snorkellers). Left out: the Dampier Archipelago map graphic (Dampier-Archipelago-Map-1.jpg); the Turtle-Watching-Code-of-Conduct graphic (text printed on it, and its tour is not in the facts file); the Sustainable Tourism badge and the TripAdvisor logo; team and guide portraits (Hal-Paine, Neri-Base-Camp, Ally-About-Us and the seven 2026/08 guide photos); the Our Partners photos and the home page Whale-Shark-1 photo, which the site describes as a tour with partner Ocean Eco Adventures, plus the Kings Ningaloo Reef Tours, Wilderness Island, Bullara and Flying Sandgroper photos, all other businesses; photos for the left-out land tours (Summer Sunset Turtle Watching, Modern History and Heritage, hire, schools and corporate); and the trekking photos on the Ningaloo Odyssey page (Ningaloo-Coastal-Trek-Opt-1, Ascending-Cape-Range-Opt, Trekking-Up-Gully-Cape-Range-Opt, Range-Camp-1-Opt), because the trek days are run by Trek Ningaloo, a different business. Two Ngajarli rock art photos (Tassie-Tiger-Ngajarli-1, Fat-Tail-Kangaroo-Ngajarli) are kept last on the Dampier trip; the site presents them as its own. The 2-Day Campout has no page, so its three photos are the home page card photo and the two photos named for the 2-dayer. No file named banner was found. No captcha or bot check was hit on any page."
